    {
      "id": "vblA",
      "code_hash": "65ad37f9b7d48d8930cfe2d0cc9b48d1",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"12-doubles-time-and-text\">12. Doubles, time and text</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Say one of your sensors sends NaN, or a timestamp lands at the far edge\nof the calendar. Integers are the easy case; real pipelines are full of\nfloating-point measurements, timestamps and strings, each with traps of\nits own, and sooner or later your data finds them. The\nLean model pins each of them down, bit for bit where it matters, because\na proof about the wrong semantics proves nothing.</span>\n<h2 id=\"doubles-nan-infinities-and-one-zero\">Doubles: NaN, infinities and one zero</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">In Python (and in IEEE 754 comparisons) <code>NaN</code> is not equal to anything,\nnot even itself, and every comparison with it is false. SQL engines\n(PostgreSQL, DuckDB, Spark, Snowflake) do something else, and so does the\nmodel: doubles are ordered</span>\n<blockquote>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">\u2212\u221e &lt; \u2026 &lt; 0.0 &lt; \u2026 &lt; +\u221e &lt; NaN</span>\n</blockquote>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">with <strong>one NaN</strong>, equal to itself and <em>larger than every number</em>, and\n<strong>one zero</strong>: IEEE's \u22120.0 <em>is</em> 0.0, so <code>-0.0 &gt;= 0</code> holds and \u22120.0 never\nshows in a result. (DataFusion's own order, <code>f64::total_cmp</code>, splits\nboth; <code>sverify</code> stores DataFusion's tables the model's way, see\n<a href=\"?file=16_datafusion_semantics.py\">chapter 16</a>.) Here it is, live:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
