    {
      "id": "PKri",
      "code_hash": "17424479e554aebe4c875b583ff9cb82",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">Two consequences that bite in practice:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>v &gt; 0</code> keeps NaN rows.</strong> A filter you meant as \"positive readings\"\n  lets glitches through. Only an upper bound (<code>v &lt; 1e9</code>, say) removes NaN.</li>\n<li><strong>\u22120.0 is 0.0</strong>: the table was given both zeros and holds one, so\n  <code>-0.0 &gt;= 0</code> is TRUE, <code>DISTINCT</code> keeps one zero, and <code>-1 * 0.0</code> is\n  <code>0.0</code>, on every engine.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Lean models doubles exactly, as integer codes in the same order\n(<code>SubstraitLean/Float.lean</code>), so the proofs know all of this. Build a\nfilter and see whether \"no NaN\" follows:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
