    {
      "id": "BYtC",
      "code_hash": "f84b8771cfae1a9acb35cbe8fdcf7175",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">Comparisons with constants are exact, including sentinels like <code>-9999.0</code>\nand boundaries like <code>v &gt; 0.5</code> versus <code>v &gt;= 0.5</code>. Arithmetic on doubles\n(<code>v * 2.5</code>) is only known to give \"some double\", because rounding isn't\nmodelled. <code>FLOOR</code>, <code>CEIL</code>, <code>ROUND</code> and the cast to an integer <em>are</em>\nexact and monotone, so a bounded double rounds to a bounded integer:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "RGSE",
